# Linux Commands Practised

# Linux Fundamentals
- `man` — View command manual
- `whoami` — Show current user
- `id` — Show user and group information

## File & Directory Management
- `pwd` — Show current directory
- `ls` — List files and directories
- `ls -a` — List hidden files and directories
- `ls -l` — List detailed file information
- `ls -ltr` — List files in detailed format sorted by modification time
- `cd` — Change directory
- `mkdir` — Create directory
- `touch` — Create file
- `rm` — Remove file
- `cp` — Copy files/directories
- `mv` — Move/rename files/directories
- `cat` — Display file contents

## Linux Filesystem
- `pwd` — Show current filesystem location
- `ls` — List filesystem contents
- `cd` — Navigate filesystem
- `ls /home` — List home directories

## User Management
- `passwd` — Change user password
- `useradd` — Create user
- `usermod` — Modify user
- `usermod -aG` — Add user to group
- `userdel` — Delete user
- `cat /etc/passwd` — View user account information
- `cat /etc/shadow` — View protected password information

## Group Management
- `groupadd` — Create group
- `groupdel` — Delete group
- `usermod -aG` — Add user to group
- `cat /etc/group` — View group information

## Password Management
- `passwd` — Change password
- `passwd -l` — Lock password
- `passwd -u` — Unlock password
- `passwd -fu` — Force unlock password

## File Permissions
- `ls -l` — View file permissions
- `chmod` — Change file permissions
- `chmod 755` — Set numeric permissions
- `chmod 644` — Set numeric permissions
- `chmod u+x` — Add execute permission for owner
- `chmod g+w` — Add write permission for group
- `chmod o-r` — Remove read permission for others

## vi Editor
- `vi` — Open file in vi editor
- `i` — Enter insert mode
- `Esc` — Return to command mode
- `:w` — Save file
- `:q` — Quit vi
- `:wq` — Save and quit
- `:q!` — Quit without saving

## Process Management
- `ps` — View running processes
- `ps aux` — View detailed process information

## Linux Networking
- `ip addr` — View IP addresses
- `ip link` — View network interfaces
- `ping` — Test network connectivity
- `hostname` — Display system hostname

## Common Linux Commands
- `echo` — Display text
- `clear` — Clear terminal
- `history` — View command history
